      OmKeyInfo.getFromProtobuf(oldPartKeyInfo.getPartKeyInfo());
  correctedSpace -= partKeyToBeDeleted.getReplicatedSize();
  RepeatedOmKeyInfo oldVerKeyInfo = getOldVersionsToCleanUp(partKeyToBeDeleted, omBucketInfo.getObjectID(),
      trxnLogIndex);
```

## Pseudo object ID and delete path key

For the overwritten part, the object ID is reused to form the delete path key. The comment explains why this is safe here, since MPU part keys always get a fresh object ID:

```java
String delKeyName = omMetadataManager.getOzoneDeletePathKey(
    partKeyToBeDeleted.getObjectID(), multipartKey);

if (!oldVerKeyInfo.getOmKeyInfoList().isEmpty()) {
  keyVersionsToDeleteMap = new HashMap<>();
  keyVersionsToDeleteMap.put(delKeyName, oldVerKeyInfo);
}
```

## wrapUncommittedBlocksAsPseudoKey usage

After quota checks and updating used bytes, the uncommitted blocks are turned into a pseudo key so they get cleaned up as an old key version, then merged into the delete map:

```java
checkBucketQuotaInBytes(omMetadataManager, omBucketInfo,
    correctedSpace);
omBucketInfo.incrUsedBytes(correctedSpace);

final OmKeyInfo pseudoKeyInfo = wrapUncommittedBlocksAsPseudoKey(uncommitted, omKeyInfo);
keyVersionsToDeleteMap = addKeyInfoToDeleteMap(ozoneManager, trxnLogIndex, ozoneKey, omBucketInfo.getObjectID(),
    pseudoKeyInfo, keyVersionsToDeleteMap);
```

The inline comment describes this as letting the uncommitted blocks "pretend as key's old version blocks."

